  "id": "DrugBank_DB12141 - MONDO_18435",
  "verbalization": "Gilteritinib, a multitargeted tyrosine kinase inhibitor, is shown to have a clinical indication for Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation and Without Maturation, both of which are associated with the same target gene, *p21* on chromosome 21. This indicates that Gilteritinib may exert its therapeutic effect on a broad spectrum of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) subtypes. The evidence suggests that Gilteritinib may act through a mechanism that targets the *p21* gene, potentially influencing cell cycle regulation and apoptosis in AML cells. The shared indication with Idarubicin, a known antileukemic agent, further supports the notion that Gilteritinib may have a role in treating AML by modulating the same biological pathways. The presence of a common target gene and the overlapping indications between Gilteritinib and Idarubicin suggest a mechanistic link, though the exact mode of action remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Gilteritinib to be repurposed in AML with *p21* mutations, provided further experimental validation confirms the direct relationship between the drug and the disease.",
